#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489345} :activity/id #uuid "f5d315b3-1937-5219-9b02-93324c4b74bb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408188 :topic/id #uuid "24a28f9b-1f4f-5349-9353-6e0a556259c4" :topic/math-academy-id 7011} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489298 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489387 :tutorial/content "It's convenient to use the following special **bar-notation** to indicate decimals that repeat indefinitely:\n\n$$\n0.\\overline{3} = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nSome decimals have two or more digits that repeat indefinitely. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{78} & =0.78\\,78\\,78\\,78… \\\\ 0.\\overset{―}{675} & =0.675\\,675\\,675…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSome repeating decimals have non-repeating digits before the repeating part. For example:\n\n$$\n0.45\\overline{67} = 0.45\\,67\\,67\\,67\\,67\\ldots\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8545445f-23ee-5309-8571-5a1f6a4b253d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15180 :tutorial/title "Notations for Repeating Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "07766a04-75bc-582b-a3f7-a4d9e94b4bfa" :step/math-academy-id 653256 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489373}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489328 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489169 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489196 :question/id #uuid "2953724f-e9c5-5a58-a253-c1d894297cc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-22734"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee93f86-38d1-591a-b694-a4f455240d75" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489282}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Rational Number as a Repeating Decimal Using Long Division"} :step/id #uuid "722917c0-0a2f-564d-92a1-3b8f90e0a367" :step/math-academy-id 653258 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489335}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489335 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489170 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489245 :question/id #uuid "975545b6-bff4-5fab-aaf4-e8ee01507788" :question/math-academy-id "e-22727"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c28dcab-dd12-58c2-88c6-0301dc0f9ce4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489222}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rational Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "84e16e8f-676c-575f-83e7-d1a743860017" :step/math-academy-id 653261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489343 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489388 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the following rational number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\n$$\n\nTo find its decimal representation, we divide $1$ by $3$ using long division, as follows:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"4\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,0 \\,. 3 {\\:\\phantom{|}} 3 {\\:\\phantom{|}} 3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\:\\ldots$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"4\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{1 {\\, . \\,} 0  {\\:\\phantom{|}} 0 {\\:\\phantom{|}} 0}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\ldots$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNotice that, in this case,\n\n- the long division process never ends, and\n\n- the digit $3$ after the decimal point repeats *forever*.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 = 0.333\\,333\\ldots\n$$\n\nThis is an example of a **repeating decimal** - a decimal in which the digits after the decimal point continue forever in a *repeating pattern*." :tutorial/id #uuid "b877953a-1821-5fae-aebf-3ff936971679" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15177 :tutorial/title "Repeating Decimals"} :step/id #uuid "989620d9-34b2-5998-bf43-7b078a5551e7" :step/math-academy-id 653255 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489298}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489345 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489385 :tutorial/content "A rational number can have a decimal representation that either *ends* or *repeats*.\n\nIn this lesson, we will see how to convert rational numbers written as fractions into decimals using *long division*. \nWe begin with examples where the decimal terminates. \n\nLet's convert $\\dfrac{7}{2}$ into a decimal using long division.\n\nTo write the fraction as a decimal, we divide the numerator by the denominator:\n\n$$\n7 \\div 2\n$$\n\nWe proceed using long division as usual, stopping once we obtain a remainder of $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"2\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,3    {\\, . \\,} 5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"2\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{7   {\\, . \\,} 0}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!\\!$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7}{2} = 3.5.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6c539020-aca7-5b8f-a2b0-b72c56336242" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15179 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9d911030-9a41-5eba-8339-d738949a2899" :step/math-academy-id 653251 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489372}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489372 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489180 :question/id #uuid "11a8381c-60e6-54f2-ac78-bece2edc403e" :question/math-academy-id "e-22733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f161693-0317-5b9e-9156-c781f703a4c0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489205} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489227} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489286}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Rational Number as a Finite Decimal Using Long Division"} :step/id #uuid "dd795099-acd6-53fe-9be5-05e82111a5a2" :step/math-academy-id 653252 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489343}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489373 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489386 :tutorial/content "The key takeaway for this lesson is that every repeating decimal is rational! In other words, every repeating decimal can be expressed as a ratio of two integers.\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{3} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0.\\overset{―}{78} & =\\frac{26}{33} \\\\ 0.333\\overset{―}{67} & =\\frac{16517}{49500}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn a future lesson, we'll learn how to express a repeating decimal as a fraction. For now, you can verify these results using a calculator." :tutorial/id #uuid "77b1162b-17a9-5251-8efe-31b7034274d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 15181 :tutorial/title "Any Repeating Decimal is a Rational"} :step/id #uuid "e016072c-e22d-536a-a6ed-5eef7793da16" :step/math-academy-id 653257 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489328}}] :activity/title "Rational Numbers as Finite or Repeating Decimals" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488466}]}
